Calcium Pyrophosphate Deposition (CPPD -Pseudogout)
Rheumatology & Immunology
Acute monoarthritis, Chondrocalcinosis, Rhomboid positively birefringent crystals

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-007
Reason for Visit: Acute, exquisitely painful monoarthritis of the right knee preventing weight-bearing and safe ambulation in an elderly patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a rapid-onset, severely swollen, and intensely hot knee joint with inability to walk. Urgent after-hours clinical assessment was absolutely necessary to rule out acute septic arthritis, which rapidly destroys articular cartilage, and to provide immediate immobilizing analgesia, as waiting until the next in-hours period posed significant risk of permanent joint destruction and falls.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee blew up like a balloon overnight; it is burning hot and even the weight of the bedsheet is agonizing."
History of Present Illness: The patient, who has a known history of advanced secondary osteoarthritis in the right knee, reports a 24-hour history of rapidly escalating symptoms. It began as a mild, deep ache yesterday afternoon. By the early hours of the morning, there was a rapid onset of joint swelling, intense erythema, and severe pain predominantly affecting the knee. The patient is completely unable to weight-bear. Notes a mild feeling of being flushed but denies frank rigors.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; unable to access the bathroom independently due to severe biomechanical restriction and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk if ambulation is attempted. Spouse unable to safely assist with transfers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient is supine, anxious, and visibly wincing upon any movement of the bed.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: Right knee demonstrates a massive, tense effusion. The overlying skin is distinctly erythematous and warm to the touch. There is exquisite tenderness over the joint line and the patella. Range of motion is severely restricted to less than 10 degrees of flexion due to localized acute inflammation and mechanical block from the effusion. Left knee shows chronic osteoarthritic changes but is cold and painless. The diagnostic tetrad is partially met clinically via acute monoarthritis and high suspicion for underlying chondrocalcinosis, pending definitive synovial fluid aspiration demonstrating rhomboid positively birefringent crystals.
Environmental Observations: Trip hazards noted on route to the bathroom; patient advised to use a urinal/bedpan temporarily.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1g taken with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Calcium Pyrophosphate Deposition (CPPD -Pseudogout) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute flare)
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high swinging fever (>38.5 C) or rigors, and history of prior similar sterile flares, though definitive exclusion requires formal joint aspiration.

Acute Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Involvement of a large joint (knee) in the presence of known established osteoarthritis, which classically favors CPPD, rather than the first MTP joint.

Acute Osteoarthritis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of marked, intense erythema, intense heat, and a tense acute effusion, which point to a crystalline inflammatory process rather than pure mechanical wear.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a hyper-acute, red, hot, and swollen monoarthritis rendering them completely immobile. Differentiating between a crystalline arthropathy and a joint-destroying septic arthritis cannot be done safely over the phone. An urgent after-hours physical examination was imperative to assess systemic toxicity, determine the necessity of an emergency joint aspirate, and safely prescribe high-dose systemic anti-inflammatories to restore functional mobility. Delaying this assessment risked severe septic complications or a catastrophic fall, directly satisfying the requirements for MBS 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate an acute course of oral corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 30mg daily for 5 days) or high-dose NSAIDs with gastric protection, pending renal function. Provide a referral for urgent knee radiography to confirm chondrocalcinosis and consider formal joint aspiration if symptoms do not rapidly improve to definitively exclude infection.
Positive Behavioral Change: Ice pack protocol: Apply a wrapped ice pack to the knee for 15 minutes every 2 hours; the immediate numbing effect and visible reduction in surface heat will actively reinforce the patient's adherence to localized anti-inflammatory care. Strict joint resting: Keeping the leg elevated and completely offloaded; avoiding the sharp, agonizing pain of weight-bearing will naturally reinforce the necessity of strict rest until medication takes effect.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedpan/urinal provided by spouse to eliminate immediate ambulation risk.
Follow-up: GP review within 24-48 hours. If symptoms worsen or fever spikes, immediate ED transfer is required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a high fever, uncontrollable shaking/chills, or if the redness begins spreading rapidly up the thigh or down the calf, call 000 or proceed to the Emergency Department immediately for a joint tap and IV antibiotics.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While this is clinically highly consistent with a "pseudogout" flare, an infection inside the joint remains a serious possibility until the swelling begins to definitively respond to medication or fluid is analyzed.
Clinical Course: With potent anti-inflammatory medication and strict rest, the acute, agonizing pain typically subsides significantly within 48 to 72 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk on this leg. If you start shivering violently, feel confused, or develop a high fever, you must go to the emergency room immediately as this could indicate a dangerous joint infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Calcium crystals have shed into your joint fluid, causing a severe immune reaction identical to an infection. The prescribed steroids will forcefully shut down this immune response and reduce the swelling so you can eventually walk again.
Actionable Steps: Take the steroids in the morning with food. Apply ice to the joint strictly for 15 minutes at a time. Use a bedside commode or bottle to pass urine tonight.

